{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the boundary-objects design note at head 68f2310c736dc06082d0fdbd7f92e8fa1a0ca169, for request 0e06e751 and review request b92d37c7. Verdict: APPROVED.\n\nThe head is one commit on 8759bbf4, which I reviewed in f9d72980. I read the note at this head first, then the diff from 8759bbf4. I checked the changed text against my seven items and against its sources at a78e0981:\n- docs/use-cases.md, groups 3 to 5 and cases 12, 25 and 50;\n- packages/scope/src/creation-versions.ts;\n- each Worker's `PLATFORM_CREATION_PINS`;\n- notes/2026-10-22-definition-succession.md.\n\n## Against review f9d72980\n\n1. Private side. Section 2 defines own room, shared room and crosses, and \"being read is not being bound\" settles what \"crosses\" means. Sections 5, 6 and 9 put a party's pile, readings and private marks in that party's own room, and recipes mount over the rooms the member reads. A source in another room appears as unreadable with its room named, which keeps section 5's third rule. Group 3 of the catalogue confirms the premise: \"Every member of a room reads every scope in that room\". Group 4 confirms that dap has no private view inside one room today. Resolved.\n2. Consumed ratification. `ratify` gains `for`, a fact reference to the opening entry of one item. The filled slot holds the member and the ratification's reference, written in the same entry as the act. So the state is one reference for each filled slot, bounded by `max`, and no guard scans history. A ratification for one item fills no slot of another item, so another member cannot spend it elsewhere. W3 now walks six presentations, and each refusal has a name. Resolved.\n3. Older rooms. An older room refuses the install with `commons-missing`. It can gain the commons only by succession, under the definition succession note, which is designed and not built. BO1 runs its witness in a new room. Resolved.\n4. Catalogue. Section 4 now names 34, 44, 45 and 46 as built, and section 7 drops the claim with no source. The new rows for cases 12 and 25 match their cards. Resolved.\n5. Counts. The catalogue pins 76 today and 77 after. The newest cohort comes from creation-versions.ts. Each Worker's setting lists six historical pins, register@17 to inbox@1, and does not change. All three match a78e0981. Resolved.\n6. Ratio. \"Six statements in seven\" is 85.7%, and the count gives 86.4%. Resolved.\n7. Three acts. Sections 7 and 9 both name `note` (a reading or a mark), `ratify` and `close`. Resolved.\n\nThe recut also takes every non-blocking point from f9d72980, and it removes the sentences that described the note rather than its subject.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree is clean at 68f2310c.\n- The diff from the base adds one file. The head merges clean onto main 4eb0956d.\n- check-doc-links on an export of the head: 712 links, 0 broken.\n- check-notes: 0 warnings.\n- check-commit-messages: 10 checked, 0 flagged.\n- `git diff --check`: clean.\n- No prose line is over 79 characters.\n- The note names no product and makes no comparison.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\nFor the slices, or a later recut of the note:\n\n1. Case 50 row. It says that \"a reviewer's comment on a draft\" stays outside the fold. The card's record keeps comments (\"campaigns, posts with their request outcomes, and comments\"). Name something the card leaves out, or say that the fold keeps a comment as text and no rule reads it.\n2. Group 3 rooms. Section 2 and section 9 call group 3's rooms own rooms. Group 3 says each party's room is shared \"with the authority\", so by section 2's own terms it is a shared room. The private side holds anyway, because the authority is the party trusted with the decision. Say \"a room of its own, shared with the authority\".\n3. List slots and cleared slots.\n   - For a party slot with `list: true`, say that the guard refuses a ratification reference that the list already holds.\n   - If an effect can clear a slot, say what stops the same ratification from being presented again.\n4. The fourth `ratified` guard, which compares the terms text's digest with the act's `terms` field, has no refusal name. The other three do.\n5. A recipe kept in a shared room names its private sources. Say that it names a `note` by its fact reference and never by the digest of its text. Otherwise the other party could confirm a short private mark, such as \"disputed\", by hashing guesses.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe note now holds together. A boundary object is one thing that both parties read, and each party also reads it in its own way. Section 9 gives the shared side a home in the shared room's commons and each party's own side a home in that party's own room. The two sides are joined by hash and by room, with no new scope kind. The locator, the three acts and the consent rule follow from sections 5 to 8. The slices BO1 to BO4 build in the order the witnesses need. Approved.\n\n## Security\n\n- Privacy rests on membership, which dap already checks on every read. The note adds no reader rule, and the unreadable source names a room and nothing else.\n- A ratification binds one item and is spent in the slot it fills, in one entry, with a bounded state.\n- `declared` slots appear on the install screen.\n- An older room refuses the install rather than running without a commons.\n- Points 3 and 5 above close two edge cases for the slices.\n\nApproved.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe recut adds one term (own room), one field (`for`) and one refusal (`commons-missing`). It adds no scope kind and no act. That is the least the seven items needed. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks:\n  - the catalogue cases in sections 3 to 5 are read correctly, except for the case 50 row (point 1);\n  - section 9 is consistent with sections 5 to 8, with the contract and with the chat-composed views note;\n  - the public-material rules hold.\n\